WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.813 --> 00:00:13.115
&gt;&gt; Good afternoon.

00:00:13.115 --> 00:00:14.701
Today we have two different speakers.

00:00:14.701 --> 00:00:17.409
We have Stewart the founder and
CEO of Slack and

00:00:17.409 --> 00:00:20.267
then we have Adam,
the Founder and CEO of Quora.

00:00:20.267 --> 00:00:21.367
I'm going to interview Stewart.

00:00:21.367 --> 00:00:24.984
We're going to talk about ideas and how to
find good ones, and then Adam is going to

00:00:24.984 --> 00:00:28.880
talk about metrics why they are important,
and what to measure.

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:31.340
Stewart, thank you for coming and
spending some time with us.

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:36.210
Stewart is the founder of Flickr and
Slack, and what I didn't know until very

00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:40.760
recently is that he tried to be
the founder twice of massively multiplayer

00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:45.090
online games, and they turned
into these two iconic companies.

00:00:45.090 --> 00:00:51.125
So I wanted to talk about just the process
of coming up with the idea for

00:00:51.125 --> 00:00:55.613
the game and then the ideas for
Flickr, and Slack.

00:00:55.613 --> 00:00:59.673
&gt;&gt; So yes, twice we set out trying to
build a web-based massively multiplayer

00:00:59.673 --> 00:01:04.050
game and the origin of that was all the
way back in 1992, I arrive at college and

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:07.411
I got my account on the school's
Unix machine and was exposed to

00:01:07.411 --> 00:01:11.492
the internet for the first time and
it was kind of a mind blowing experience.

00:01:11.492 --> 00:01:14.813
So, this is about a year
before the web became popular.

00:01:14.813 --> 00:01:20.698
So it was like IRC, HughesNet, talk, email
and I was really fascinated with the idea

00:01:20.698 --> 00:01:26.374
that people could find their community
irrespective of what that community was.

00:01:26.374 --> 00:01:31.646
So if you were a breast cancer survivor or
a model train enthusiast or whatever,

00:01:31.646 --> 00:01:36.604
if you were interested in some as I
was obscure 19th century biologists,

00:01:36.604 --> 00:01:38.827
then you can find all that stuff.

00:01:38.827 --> 00:01:44.615
And the idea of a game that lived online
like that and had the game like aspects or

00:01:44.615 --> 00:01:49.892
play as a pretext for social
interactions seemed really interesting.

00:01:49.892 --> 00:01:53.155
And I'm not going to go into to
long of an explanation, because it

00:01:53.155 --> 00:01:57.477
turns out it's like a terrible, terrible
idea at least as a commercial business.

00:01:57.477 --> 00:02:01.224
Like it might have been fun as a hobby,
I think it could have been the kind of

00:02:01.224 --> 00:02:04.982
business that sustained the number
of employees required to operate it.

00:02:04.982 --> 00:02:08.698
So, like a restaurant or
something like that.

00:02:08.698 --> 00:02:12.694
But when in 2009, we started
the company that ended up making Slack,

00:02:12.694 --> 00:02:16.303
we raised $17.5 million in extra
capital and it's just never

00:02:16.303 --> 00:02:20.311
going to become the kind of business
that would justify that investment.

00:02:20.311 --> 00:02:22.017
The first time was a little bit different.

00:02:22.017 --> 00:02:26.104
So, 2002 was after the Dot.com crash.

00:02:26.104 --> 00:02:28.631
It was after WorldCom and
Enron accounting scandals.

00:02:28.631 --> 00:02:33.265
It was after 9/11 and
the NASDAQ was down 80%.

00:02:33.265 --> 00:02:35.841
S&amp;P 500 was down, 65% from its peak.

00:02:35.841 --> 00:02:38.593
No one wanted to invest
in internet facing stuff.

00:02:38.593 --> 00:02:42.951
So it was very hard to tell whether
this was a good idea or not,

00:02:42.951 --> 00:02:46.882
because there was no signal
from the market that said,

00:02:46.882 --> 00:02:52.279
this is an investable idea, because
nothing was investable at that time.

00:02:52.279 --> 00:02:54.718
We raised a little bit of friends and
family money.

00:02:54.718 --> 00:02:58.245
We spent about a year or
so building a prototype.

00:02:58.245 --> 00:03:01.658
The prototype was well-received, but
it was going to take us another year and

00:03:01.658 --> 00:03:04.284
a half to finish it and
we couldn't raise any money at all.

00:03:04.284 --> 00:03:07.603
We got to the point where the one person
on the team who had kids against was

00:03:07.603 --> 00:03:11.251
the only person that's getting paid and
then we decided to try to find something

00:03:11.251 --> 00:03:14.954
else we could do that would make use of
the technology that we already developed,

00:03:14.954 --> 00:03:16.880
but that will be quicker to get to market.

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:21.107
And that thing was Flicker, but
it was also a bad idea at first.

00:03:21.107 --> 00:03:26.038
So because we had developed this game
client and we had a messaging server on

00:03:26.038 --> 00:03:31.372
the back-end, we just re-purposed the game
client as a photo sharing client.

00:03:31.372 --> 00:03:34.562
So in the game you had a bunch of slots
along the bottom of the screen for

00:03:34.562 --> 00:03:37.489
inventory, those became slots
into which you can fit photos.

00:03:37.489 --> 00:03:40.097
And because the game was
all real time interaction,

00:03:40.097 --> 00:03:43.949
the photo sharing was all real-time
interaction which meant that you have to

00:03:43.949 --> 00:03:47.461
be online at the same time as someone
you cared about in order to use it.

00:03:47.461 --> 00:03:52.093
And while it demoed really well,
it was amazing technology, especially for

00:03:52.093 --> 00:03:55.529
at the time we started and
that maybe like late 2003.

00:03:55.529 --> 00:03:57.150
It was a terrible product,

00:03:57.150 --> 00:04:01.600
because you didn't care about any of
the people who were actually online.

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:03.907
That morphed really quickly and

00:04:03.907 --> 00:04:08.016
the experience making Slack
was a little bit different.

00:04:08.016 --> 00:04:10.577
We had all of the resources
we could ever ask for.

00:04:10.577 --> 00:04:15.309
I mean, we had more than enough money,
because we had money leftover and

00:04:15.309 --> 00:04:20.133
we decided to abandon the idea and
we had AWS which we didn't have before.

00:04:20.133 --> 00:04:23.245
Hardware was effectively free internet
connections were much better.

00:04:23.245 --> 00:04:24.621
There were many more people online.

00:04:24.621 --> 00:04:26.081
Hardware was better.

00:04:26.081 --> 00:04:29.744
So, none of like those external
conditions was the reason this wasn't.

00:04:29.744 --> 00:04:32.508
This massively multiplayer game
on the web wasn't a good idea.

00:04:32.508 --> 00:04:35.398
It just wasn't a good idea, because
there wasn't a big enough market for it.

00:04:35.398 --> 00:04:37.671
&gt;&gt; And why was Slack a good idea and

00:04:37.671 --> 00:04:41.355
how did you decide that
Slack was a thing to build?

00:04:41.355 --> 00:04:44.650
&gt;&gt; Well,
we started developing the system for

00:04:44.650 --> 00:04:50.438
internal communication that was base IRC
and those of you who had IRC kind of,

00:04:50.438 --> 00:04:55.423
and also use Slack can recognize
that Slack is IRC with all of things

00:04:55.423 --> 00:05:00.258
that were missing when it was
originally designed in 1989.

00:05:00.258 --> 00:05:03.574
I think this is, I don't know,
it's like it's post facto analysis.

00:05:03.574 --> 00:05:05.968
So it's hard to know if this
is really true or not, but

00:05:05.968 --> 00:05:09.856
I think that the reason this lock has been
so successful is because we had a 3.5-year

00:05:09.856 --> 00:05:13.289
long design process where we weren't
conscious that we were designing it.

00:05:13.289 --> 00:05:14.994
So, we started using IRC.

00:05:14.994 --> 00:05:16.713
IRC has many deficiencies.

00:05:16.713 --> 00:05:19.023
Among them, you can't store and
forward the messages.

00:05:19.023 --> 00:05:22.236
So if I wanted to send you a message and
you're not connected to the server that

00:05:22.236 --> 00:05:24.771
the moment I want to send it,
I just can't send you a message.

00:05:24.771 --> 00:05:27.596
So, the first thing we built was
about to log all the messages and

00:05:27.596 --> 00:05:28.705
put them in our archive.

00:05:28.705 --> 00:05:32.127
And once we had that,
we had messages at a database and

00:05:32.127 --> 00:05:37.460
we thought search would be useful, so
we built search for it and this was 2009.

00:05:37.460 --> 00:05:39.902
There were no good IRC clients for
iPhones.

00:05:39.902 --> 00:05:44.236
And so, we built an HTML5 front-end for
the archives and Safari.

00:05:44.236 --> 00:05:47.589
And once we had that, we wanted to be able
to post and just like on and on and on.

00:05:47.589 --> 00:05:50.802
But in the normal process
of software development,

00:05:50.802 --> 00:05:53.730
there's a huge amount of ego and
speculation.

00:05:53.730 --> 00:05:58.297
So when you're arguing with someone about
whether this feature is a good idea or

00:05:58.297 --> 00:06:02.315
not and if you can get to the point
of enlightenment and a Buddha sense,

00:06:02.315 --> 00:06:04.789
then maybe you can do
that without any ego.

00:06:04.789 --> 00:06:09.377
But the practical reality is people are
very ego laden in those discussions and

00:06:09.377 --> 00:06:14.539
there's a lot of speculation like, I think
this could be really valuable for someone.

00:06:14.539 --> 00:06:19.006
Whereas when we were building the system
that was kind of the proto Slack,

00:06:19.006 --> 00:06:24.213
that was the inspiration for Slack, it was
whatever problem is the most irritating.

00:06:24.213 --> 00:06:27.442
Like something that we just absolutely
can't stand, we would address that and

00:06:27.442 --> 00:06:30.670
then with a minimum amount of effort and
then go back to what we're supposed to be

00:06:30.670 --> 00:06:32.760
doing and then kind of let cook for
several months.

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:37.030
So we had 45 people working on it,
working on the game.

00:06:37.030 --> 00:06:41.763
So, we had a lot of real world feedback or
when there was a problem.

00:06:41.763 --> 00:06:44.361
I mean, when an opportunity that was so
obvious that we couldn't help, but

00:06:44.361 --> 00:06:47.001
take advantage of it, we would spend
the minimum number of minutes on that and

00:06:47.001 --> 00:06:47.506
go back to it.

00:06:47.506 --> 00:06:52.628
So the end of that process, we had this
fully designed product that we were using

00:06:52.628 --> 00:06:57.444
that was a terrible implementation not
what you would do if you started from

00:06:57.444 --> 00:07:02.504
scratch, but it was obvious that this
was something that had enormous value.

00:07:02.504 --> 00:07:05.548
Everyone who worked at the company agreed
they would never work without something

00:07:05.548 --> 00:07:06.218
like this again.

00:07:06.218 --> 00:07:08.985
And so just on that basis,
it seemed like it was a great idea and

00:07:08.985 --> 00:07:10.167
we had years of evidence.

00:07:10.167 --> 00:07:12.198
&gt;&gt; Were there other things
you considered pivoting to or

00:07:12.198 --> 00:07:14.960
was it just like there's this thing
we're going to do it, it's working.

00:07:14.960 --> 00:07:19.664
&gt;&gt; I wish that I had better access to the
proposals that I personally had written,

00:07:19.664 --> 00:07:24.651
ciz I had a whole bunch of **** terrible,
terrible ideas and this was the one that

00:07:24.651 --> 00:07:29.731
had the broader consensus among the team
as being something that would be valuable.

00:07:29.731 --> 00:07:33.963
What's interesting at the time though,
so we had $5 million left in the bank.

00:07:33.963 --> 00:07:35.582
None of our investors
wanted their money back.

00:07:35.582 --> 00:07:37.829
They want us to just go
try something else and

00:07:37.829 --> 00:07:40.805
Jason Horowitz is one of our investors,
so we went down and

00:07:40.805 --> 00:07:44.298
did the partner meeting and
kind of presented what our big idea was.

00:07:44.298 --> 00:07:48.331
And at that time, this is before we
actually started development of Slack.

00:07:48.331 --> 00:07:51.789
But when we had this proto version,
we said that one day,

00:07:51.789 --> 00:07:55.986
this could be like a hundred million
dollar a year revenue business.

00:07:55.986 --> 00:07:59.380
It could be valued at a billion dollars
on that basis and we thought that,

00:07:59.380 --> 00:08:02.231
that was like a decade out and
that would be the culmination.

00:08:02.231 --> 00:08:07.170
That would be We have 100% of the ten and

00:08:07.170 --> 00:08:10.820
we went past that in year two,
kind of blew past it.

00:08:10.820 --> 00:08:13.940
We'll do 200 million in
gap revenue this year and

00:08:13.940 --> 00:08:16.190
still be growing at a very fast rate.

00:08:16.190 --> 00:08:20.790
And now we think that number is
more like 10 billion in revenue.

00:08:20.790 --> 00:08:23.140
Like a hundred billion dollar company.

00:08:23.140 --> 00:08:26.140
So we didn't realize how
good of an idea it was, but

00:08:26.140 --> 00:08:28.610
it's also there's an interesting
story about deciding to shut it down.

00:08:28.610 --> 00:08:34.570
Because we still had money, because we had
a relatively big user base who were very

00:08:34.570 --> 00:08:40.160
engaged, but we had a really like a leaky
bucket, it was very difficult to add

00:08:40.160 --> 00:08:45.180
new users to the game that we're
working on, which is called Glitch.

00:08:45.180 --> 00:08:47.090
I felt very stuck in this position,

00:08:47.090 --> 00:08:53.410
because on the one hand you have narrative
of good entrepeneurs are resilient.

00:08:53.410 --> 00:08:56.550
When everyone else thinks it's a bad idea,
it's probably a really good idea.

00:08:56.550 --> 00:08:58.960
You have to dig in, prove people wrong.

00:08:58.960 --> 00:09:00.960
Keep going, even when times are dark.

00:09:00.960 --> 00:09:04.110
One the other hand,
there's a morning when I woke up and

00:09:04.110 --> 00:09:07.300
it's just like,
I can't do this anymore, I'm the CEO,

00:09:07.300 --> 00:09:10.160
I'm the chairman of the board,
I'm sure that this is not going to work.

00:09:10.160 --> 00:09:12.050
And it's actually hard to
convince everyone else.

00:09:12.050 --> 00:09:15.740
&gt;&gt; I think that's one of the hardest
things about evaluating ideas.

00:09:15.740 --> 00:09:17.050
Is saying when this
one's not going to work.

00:09:17.050 --> 00:09:19.850
So, do you remember what it was about
that morning when you woke up and said,

00:09:19.850 --> 00:09:22.690
you know what, it's time to move on?

00:09:24.310 --> 00:09:28.120
&gt;&gt; I don't.
I mean, I think people have very little

00:09:28.120 --> 00:09:30.650
interest in access to their own
cognitive processes to begin with.

00:09:30.650 --> 00:09:33.440
So anything say would be
kind of making it up.

00:09:33.440 --> 00:09:38.480
But I think it was I had exhausted all
the ideas we had to fix problems, so

00:09:38.480 --> 00:09:41.360
we focused on new user experience.

00:09:41.360 --> 00:09:45.170
And we really wanted to teach
people what this was and

00:09:45.170 --> 00:09:48.090
kind of what the possibilities were and
get them excited about it.

00:09:48.090 --> 00:09:50.760
Because we were okay at attracting
people into the top of the funnel but

00:09:50.760 --> 00:09:52.700
had a hard time retaining them.

00:09:52.700 --> 00:09:53.660
And.

00:09:53.660 --> 00:09:56.410
&gt;&gt; That's always when I tell people to
give up an idea when you're out of ideas

00:09:56.410 --> 00:09:57.530
about how to make it work.

00:09:57.530 --> 00:10:00.870
&gt;&gt; Yeah exactly, so we'd have an idea,
try it didn't work, have another idea and

00:10:00.870 --> 00:10:06.990
at first you can fill up post it notes,
like those giant boards and you can fill

00:10:06.990 --> 00:10:09.780
up white boards with ideas and you argue
about which ones are the highest value.

00:10:09.780 --> 00:10:12.880
And then one by one you eliminate them.

00:10:12.880 --> 00:10:16.910
Months go by, a year goes by,
another six months goes by.

00:10:16.910 --> 00:10:20.570
And it's like ****, these are all of the
ideas we thought were the worst ones to

00:10:20.570 --> 00:10:24.580
begin with and we only have three left so
this is probably not going to work.

00:10:25.650 --> 00:10:28.240
&gt;&gt; You mentioned at one point
you had a bunch of proposals,

00:10:28.240 --> 00:10:31.050
how do you come up with ideas?

00:10:31.050 --> 00:10:32.340
Or how did you even think that you know,

00:10:32.340 --> 00:10:35.130
slack is maybe this thing
is a really good idea.

00:10:35.130 --> 00:10:38.550
How do you come up with new ideas and
how do you evaluate them?

00:10:38.550 --> 00:10:39.880
&gt;&gt; Coming up with new ideas is easy.

00:10:39.880 --> 00:10:44.840
Like I just I feel like it's hard
almost to not come up with ideas.

00:10:44.840 --> 00:10:47.660
I mean,
they're not necessarily good ideas so

00:10:47.660 --> 00:10:50.720
the recognition of which ones
are good is actually much harder.

00:10:50.720 --> 00:10:56.390
I recently finally got around
to reading thinking fast and

00:10:56.390 --> 00:11:00.500
Slow and there's this story there of
a psychologist named Herbert Simon

00:11:00.500 --> 00:11:05.300
who studied in Chess Grand Masters
because Chess Grand Masters

00:11:05.300 --> 00:11:09.190
are one of the category of people
like really experienced doctors.

00:11:09.190 --> 00:11:13.840
That people regard as sort of miraculous
in their ability to intuit a situation,

00:11:13.840 --> 00:11:17.770
like glance at a board and say,
white checkmate in three moves.

00:11:17.770 --> 00:11:21.810
And people thought that this was like
some super well developed intuition.

00:11:23.220 --> 00:11:28.530
And Professor Simon argues that it's just
a recognition in the same way that like

00:11:28.530 --> 00:11:31.490
a 18 month old can look at a dog and
say doggie.

00:11:31.490 --> 00:11:36.290
Or within the first 500 milliseconds of
someone speaking you can tell if they're

00:11:36.290 --> 00:11:41.000
angry at you, like you just develop
the ability to recognize situation and

00:11:41.000 --> 00:11:42.040
ques which.

00:11:42.040 --> 00:11:46.720
Are hard to disambiguate, they're hard to
articulate and enumerate in your mind and

00:11:46.720 --> 00:11:48.610
say exactly why you think this way.

00:11:48.610 --> 00:11:50.480
But over time, you get better and
better doing it.

00:11:50.480 --> 00:11:53.485
I've been making software
professionally for about 20 years, so

00:11:53.485 --> 00:11:58.310
the specific micro-ideas, or sub-ideas,

00:11:58.310 --> 00:12:02.670
about feature implementation, or the order
of actions in the user experience.

00:12:02.670 --> 00:12:06.100
Or whether a certain feature is more
important than another feature.

00:12:06.100 --> 00:12:08.830
Those ones,
I feel like I have an intuitive sense.

00:12:08.830 --> 00:12:11.550
Which is actually just the ability
to recognize, over time.

00:12:11.550 --> 00:12:13.510
So it's really just
a process of filtering.

00:12:13.510 --> 00:12:15.680
Like you just have constant ideas.

00:12:15.680 --> 00:12:16.690
Everyone has ideas.

00:12:16.690 --> 00:12:21.690
We at Slack have developed a lot
of organizational design and

00:12:21.690 --> 00:12:24.720
practices to try to
absorb as many as we can.

00:12:24.720 --> 00:12:29.330
So we get about, somewhere between 10 and
20,000 tweets at us a month.

00:12:29.330 --> 00:12:34.260
And all of those are responded
to by a actual human being.

00:12:34.260 --> 00:12:39.240
We get a similar number of customer
support tickets, and we have Processes for

00:12:39.240 --> 00:12:45.030
trying to filter up and abstract all
of the feedback that we're getting.

00:12:45.030 --> 00:12:48.480
But even down to the level
of all the tweets at

00:12:48.480 --> 00:12:52.190
Slack the company are pumped into our
Slack instance and people check them, and

00:12:52.190 --> 00:12:54.940
they can add an emoji reaction and
that filters it to another channel.

00:12:56.510 --> 00:13:00.460
For the ones that seem above some
threshold of this seems like a good idea,

00:13:00.460 --> 00:13:02.120
and it also seems practical.

00:13:02.120 --> 00:13:04.490
I could imagine that we could do it.

00:13:04.490 --> 00:13:09.368
And then there's some debate at
that level, but, I mean, every

00:13:11.239 --> 00:13:15.330
Well, because almost every person I know
in the technology industry also use Slack.

00:13:15.330 --> 00:13:18.390
Every time I talk to any of
them they have ideas for me.

00:13:18.390 --> 00:13:23.690
I mean they have complains or proposals
&gt;&gt; So

00:13:23.690 --> 00:13:26.900
the big ideas or
the little ideas I get the intuition on.

00:13:26.900 --> 00:13:30.330
What about the big idea, did you spend
a lot of time thinking before you

00:13:30.330 --> 00:13:34.140
decided to go ahead with Slack, did you
spend a lot of time thinking about this

00:13:34.140 --> 00:13:37.780
would be really strategically valuable and
there would be a deep network effect?

00:13:37.780 --> 00:13:38.410
&gt;&gt; No not at all.

00:13:38.410 --> 00:13:41.180
I mean so we thought that this would
be valuable for individual teams and

00:13:41.180 --> 00:13:44.310
we could see that value
demonstrated in our own experience.

00:13:45.450 --> 00:13:49.840
We definitely didn't see
how applicable it would be.

00:13:49.840 --> 00:13:54.385
Because right now, we have 38,000
paid customers around the world, so

00:13:54.385 --> 00:13:55.035
that's a lot.

00:13:55.035 --> 00:13:59.705
We have a sales team but we've never
talked to 90 plus percent of our customers

00:13:59.705 --> 00:14:02.715
because we just couldn't
talk to that many people.

00:14:02.715 --> 00:14:07.007
So again, this is already farther than
I thought we would get at the very end.

00:14:07.007 --> 00:14:09.627
And so we under recognize it.

00:14:09.627 --> 00:14:11.837
Those big ideas are, I don't know,

00:14:11.837 --> 00:14:17.827
we talked briefly about this topic
before and one of my favorite lines is,

00:14:17.827 --> 00:14:21.220
I have a great idea for a novel all I
need is for someone to write it for me.

00:14:21.220 --> 00:14:22.710
Because obviously preposterous.

00:14:22.710 --> 00:14:25.140
Right?
Like no one would really think,

00:14:25.140 --> 00:14:28.920
I mean only a really deluded person
would think that that's true.

00:14:29.920 --> 00:14:32.030
But people definitely have ideas for

00:14:32.030 --> 00:14:36.480
apps all the time, and
those can be harder to assess.

00:14:36.480 --> 00:14:41.290
And it's so rare that I hear one where
someone proposes an idea where it just,

00:14:41.290 --> 00:14:45.952
it's like prima facie that is a good
idea as opposed to [SOUND] I don't know,

00:14:45.952 --> 00:14:48.617
I mean it really depends on the execution.

00:14:48.617 --> 00:14:52.548
Actually just last night,
I was watching something on YouTube, and

00:14:52.548 --> 00:14:57.084
the ad that came up before was for an app
called MileIQ, and it's you turn it on,

00:14:57.084 --> 00:15:01.148
ask for location permissions, and
then every time you go on a car ride,

00:15:01.148 --> 00:15:04.960
it just looks to see the speed
that you're traveling.

00:15:04.960 --> 00:15:07.950
And then at the point you stop,
it stops it.

00:15:07.950 --> 00:15:09.550
So kind of like an automatic Strava.

00:15:09.550 --> 00:15:10.920
Like it just records all your trips.

00:15:12.080 --> 00:15:15.200
And then you can swipe right to say that

00:15:15.200 --> 00:15:19.150
this is a trip that my company's supposed
to reimburse me for and swipe left, not.

00:15:19.150 --> 00:15:20.800
Anyway, it's a very simple idea.

00:15:20.800 --> 00:15:23.520
It's actually a really great idea because
people have trouble recording those,

00:15:23.520 --> 00:15:28.580
they have trouble in audits with the IRS
to prove that they used the card for

00:15:28.580 --> 00:15:29.810
this purpose and stuff like that.

00:15:29.810 --> 00:15:31.870
And that's one that actually seems like.

00:15:31.870 --> 00:15:34.180
Hm, the execution doesn't
seem very difficult.

00:15:34.180 --> 00:15:36.040
I think that would be a good idea.

00:15:36.040 --> 00:15:38.980
If you could market it effectively, it
would probably be a successful business.

00:15:38.980 --> 00:15:43.950
But like 95% of the ideas I hear,
I have no idea whether it'd be successful.

00:15:43.950 --> 00:15:47.130
&gt;&gt; Where are you generally on
the spectrum of ideas matter and

00:15:47.130 --> 00:15:48.660
it's worth the effort to
think of a good one and

00:15:48.660 --> 00:15:51.580
I think it's just all that execution,
and ideas don't matter at all.

00:15:51.580 --> 00:15:54.500
And you just have to keep
trying things until someday

00:15:54.500 --> 00:15:56.068
one of your internal
tools turns into slack.

00:15:56.068 --> 00:15:59.602
&gt;&gt; So there's,

00:15:59.602 --> 00:16:04.470
I'm There's

00:16:04.470 --> 00:16:07.630
a lot of people in this class and then
there's a lot of people at Stanford, and

00:16:07.630 --> 00:16:11.850
there's a lot of people in South Bay and
there's a lot of people in the Bay area

00:16:11.850 --> 00:16:16.460
and many many people want to start tech
companies and all hope to be successful.

00:16:16.460 --> 00:16:20.710
So to a certain extent there is like 100
million monkeys banging on typewriters and

00:16:20.710 --> 00:16:24.030
someone's going to start a Shakespeare
sonnet so, there is a little bit of that.

00:16:24.030 --> 00:16:25.920
Like a little bit of survivorship bias and

00:16:25.920 --> 00:16:28.200
other cognitive biases
that come into play.

00:16:28.200 --> 00:16:31.240
On the other hand, I don't think
either end of the spectrums is right.

00:16:31.240 --> 00:16:33.280
I would definitely lean
more towards execution.

00:16:33.280 --> 00:16:38.770
There's a good Steve Jobs elaboration
of John Sculley got hired and

00:16:38.770 --> 00:16:44.430
started hiring all these people at Apple
who thought that only the ideas matter.

00:16:44.430 --> 00:16:46.520
The idea absolutely matters, but

00:16:46.520 --> 00:16:51.750
the idea when you start hitting execution
has to accommodate so many things.

00:16:51.750 --> 00:16:56.690
In Steve Jobs case, it's about what
it's possible to get glass to do and

00:16:56.690 --> 00:17:01.400
how small you can make batteries and what
kind of tensile strength plastics have,

00:17:01.400 --> 00:17:02.280
and stuff like that.

00:17:02.280 --> 00:17:07.440
because your idea has to stand up to all
these things and it'll transmogrify and

00:17:07.440 --> 00:17:10.740
alter and may even be unrecognizable
at the end, but if you're really

00:17:10.740 --> 00:17:15.510
good at execution and the core of the idea
can make it through all those steps.

00:17:16.860 --> 00:17:20.870
So I absolutely favor execution,
but you can execute

00:17:21.940 --> 00:17:26.680
a lot on a terrible idea and then not
get anywhere so you kind of need both.

00:17:26.680 --> 00:17:31.710
&gt;&gt; If you were starting
a new company today,

00:17:31.710 --> 00:17:34.920
you know April 2017,
where would you look for ideas?

00:17:34.920 --> 00:17:36.590
What sorts of things would
you be thinking about?

00:17:41.610 --> 00:17:43.590
&gt;&gt; I don't know if this works for
other people, but

00:17:43.590 --> 00:17:47.454
I would definitely look at my own
experiences as a consumer, generally.

00:17:47.454 --> 00:17:52.800
Because it's very easy for
me to see things that are frustrating,

00:17:52.800 --> 00:17:59.670
I complain about stuff a lot like,
especially given how good my life is.

00:17:59.670 --> 00:18:02.780
My life is really good and yet
every time I have to fly somewhere I have

00:18:03.840 --> 00:18:07.770
at least ten significant complaints and
50 minor complaints.

00:18:07.770 --> 00:18:09.670
And every one of those is an opportunity,
right?

00:18:09.670 --> 00:18:14.480
Every one of those things
that don't go as well

00:18:14.480 --> 00:18:19.480
as you would like are opportunities for
improvement and some of those seem like

00:18:19.480 --> 00:18:22.970
someone else should see them and
it should be really obvious.

00:18:22.970 --> 00:18:24.150
But they're often not.

00:18:24.150 --> 00:18:29.850
There's this story I tell in our
internal on boarding process of me and

00:18:29.850 --> 00:18:32.940
our head of product design going for
a walk in Vancouver.

00:18:32.940 --> 00:18:36.760
And our office in Vancouver is in
a neighborhood that has really narrow

00:18:36.760 --> 00:18:37.480
sidewalks.

00:18:37.480 --> 00:18:40.042
The sidewalks have sandwich
boards from vendors out so

00:18:40.042 --> 00:18:42.370
it's very crowded so
you kind of wind around.

00:18:42.370 --> 00:18:46.100
And it starts raining
while we're on this walk.

00:18:46.100 --> 00:18:48.680
And maybe two thirds of the people had
umbrellas with them and we didn't and

00:18:48.680 --> 00:18:50.830
people are walking towards
us on the sidewalk.

00:18:50.830 --> 00:18:54.620
And almost no one would move
their umbrella out of the way so

00:18:54.620 --> 00:18:57.230
that the pokey things
wouldn't get us in the eye.

00:18:57.230 --> 00:19:00.470
And like I said, the sidewalks are very
narrow, so we had to keep on ducking.

00:19:01.550 --> 00:19:04.290
And I can come up with many
explanations for why this would be.

00:19:04.290 --> 00:19:09.330
But don't explain with malice that
which can be explained by ignorance.

00:19:09.330 --> 00:19:12.720
So it probably wasn't just that
these people have few avenues for

00:19:12.720 --> 00:19:14.000
exercising power in their lives,

00:19:14.000 --> 00:19:17.480
and they were choosing like this
moment to exercise some power.

00:19:17.480 --> 00:19:19.010
But then there's only
really two explanations.

00:19:19.010 --> 00:19:20.900
One is,
they just walk through the world and

00:19:20.900 --> 00:19:23.930
they don't see that we're going like
this to get away from their umbrella,

00:19:23.930 --> 00:19:27.310
even though inevitably they've
had that experience themselves.

00:19:27.310 --> 00:19:29.300
Or they see that this is happening and
they're like,

00:19:29.300 --> 00:19:33.020
I just can't think of anything I
can do to ameliorate the situation,

00:19:33.020 --> 00:19:38.040
despite the fact that it's this tiny,
like a hundredth of a calorie,

00:19:38.040 --> 00:19:41.590
worth of effort and
a tiny amount of consideration.

00:19:41.590 --> 00:19:46.260
And the point of telling that story is
that that's the way, and this is a sad way

00:19:46.260 --> 00:19:48.810
of looking at the world, but that's the
way that most people go through the world.

00:19:48.810 --> 00:19:52.740
They're oblivious to the problems that
other people have and If they notice

00:19:52.740 --> 00:19:56.230
the problems, they're unable to
come up with any kind of solution.

00:19:56.230 --> 00:20:00.110
So like two thirds of the people
just didn't tilt their umbrellas.

00:20:00.110 --> 00:20:02.660
Which means that if you're the kind
of person who's willing to tilt your

00:20:02.660 --> 00:20:05.390
umbrella, there's a whole world
of opportunity out there.

00:20:05.390 --> 00:20:08.501
&gt;&gt; Do you have any tips on how to notice
that you should tilt your umbrella?

00:20:10.020 --> 00:20:11.100
&gt;&gt; Pay attention.

00:20:11.100 --> 00:20:13.930
I mean, that is the biggest thing.

00:20:13.930 --> 00:20:17.010
But pay attention
especially to other people.

00:20:17.010 --> 00:20:19.200
So you can pay attention
to your own signals and

00:20:19.200 --> 00:20:23.100
maybe if you get practiced-
&gt;&gt; This has actually worked

00:20:23.100 --> 00:20:26.740
at the [INAUDIBLE] history when people
have needed to come up with a new idea

00:20:26.740 --> 00:20:29.630
on the fly, it doesn't usually work,
but when they really just commit to

00:20:29.630 --> 00:20:34.230
paying attention to other people and sort
of what seems broken, that has worked.

00:20:36.260 --> 00:20:40.780
And then so if you do it this way you
come up with like thousands of ideas.

00:20:40.780 --> 00:20:44.330
Anything you've noticed as a good way to
filter those besides just building up

00:20:44.330 --> 00:20:48.210
the experience of seeing a lot and
the recognition?

00:20:48.210 --> 00:20:51.150
&gt;&gt; Yeah there is the kind of
dialectic between execution and

00:20:51.150 --> 00:20:55.990
idea because there's definitely
something to be said

00:20:57.000 --> 00:21:03.180
when things are above some threshold
of good to sticking with the same idea.

00:21:03.180 --> 00:21:07.940
Not so much because that idea's
the best one, but because the wild and

00:21:07.940 --> 00:21:10.490
erratic chasing of the next idea

00:21:10.490 --> 00:21:14.210
when you have something that has a little
bit of traction can be really dangerous.

00:21:14.210 --> 00:21:17.130
So, Slack is a good example of that.

00:21:17.130 --> 00:21:20.750
We conceived of Slack as a tool for
business.

00:21:20.750 --> 00:21:24.490
We developed it that way,
we priced it that way, it was successful.

00:21:24.490 --> 00:21:29.010
But, there's also this huge
amount of social usage, like now,

00:21:29.010 --> 00:21:33.090
maybe a third of the Slack teams that get
created are for some kind of social usage.

00:21:33.090 --> 00:21:37.160
And a lot of people had the idea
that we should pursue that,

00:21:37.160 --> 00:21:43.120
we should make Slack for groups, or even
just pivot to being a consumer company,

00:21:43.120 --> 00:21:46.550
like kind of a private version of
Twitter or something like that.

00:21:46.550 --> 00:21:50.960
And we've been pretty steadfast in not
doing that because you can only do so

00:21:50.960 --> 00:21:52.140
many things well.

00:21:52.140 --> 00:21:58.460
And there's a lot of shiny objects
like that, that are very distracted.

00:21:58.460 --> 00:22:01.090
&gt;&gt; Are you the one that says no
to most of those new ideas or

00:22:01.090 --> 00:22:02.550
you have you had to build
that into the company?

00:22:02.550 --> 00:22:05.269
When something's going as well as Slack,
there's so many new ideas.

00:22:06.550 --> 00:22:08.880
&gt;&gt; I'm chief no sayer but

00:22:08.880 --> 00:22:12.760
there's definitely a whole bunch of
people who are equally committed to no.

00:22:12.760 --> 00:22:15.330
&gt;&gt; Great.
We can probably take one question for

00:22:15.330 --> 00:22:17.890
Stewart if someone has
a really good one before-

00:22:17.890 --> 00:22:18.920
&gt;&gt; Only a really, really good one.

00:22:18.920 --> 00:22:22.687
&gt;&gt; Maybe that was so
much pressure now no one's going to ask.

00:22:22.687 --> 00:22:24.438
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So

00:22:24.438 --> 00:22:27.920
you already had a successful
company before Slack.

00:22:27.920 --> 00:22:31.015
I was wondering what the differences
between as a first time founder and

00:22:31.015 --> 00:22:32.080
a second time founder?

00:22:32.080 --> 00:22:34.780
&gt;&gt; What are the differences
as a second time founder?

00:22:34.780 --> 00:22:41.240
&gt;&gt; So Everyone of the external
factors was much easier.

00:22:41.240 --> 00:22:46.130
So the first time I mentioned that
we ended up making Flickr because we

00:22:46.130 --> 00:22:49.150
couldn't raise any money and we're
desperate to try to finish something.

00:22:50.580 --> 00:22:54.370
Fast forward seven years, and
we could raise as much money as we wanted.

00:22:54.370 --> 00:22:55.530
So that was easy.

00:22:55.530 --> 00:22:59.260
We didn't even make a DAC, we just said,
we would like some money and

00:22:59.260 --> 00:23:02.010
then people gave us money.

00:23:02.010 --> 00:23:05.137
So a lot of those things were easier,
it's always easier to get press,

00:23:05.137 --> 00:23:08.380
it's easier get attention,
easier to recruit, easier to raise money.

00:23:11.820 --> 00:23:16.199
It's not any easier to be successful
certainly because we started the company

00:23:16.199 --> 00:23:20.840
that made Slack in 2009 and failed
pretty hard for three and a half years.

00:23:20.840 --> 00:23:24.420
Kind of like pushing into
the wind of something that wasn't

00:23:24.420 --> 00:23:25.570
ultimately going to work.

00:23:25.570 --> 00:23:30.210
And we could have just stopped there.

00:23:30.210 --> 00:23:32.740
It was great for
investors that we didn't recap and

00:23:32.740 --> 00:23:38.530
we just kept the same company and so
that company has now been successful.

00:23:38.530 --> 00:23:40.810
We could've also just walked
away from that company and

00:23:40.810 --> 00:23:41.710
incorporated a new company.

00:23:41.710 --> 00:23:44.820
And then it would've been my second
company was a failure instead of

00:23:44.820 --> 00:23:46.605
my second company was a great pivot.

00:23:49.830 --> 00:23:54.790
I'm not sure which things get harder,
I'm not sure than any do other than

00:23:56.450 --> 00:24:01.530
the degree of self criticism and
like awareness of your own limitations.

00:24:03.130 --> 00:24:04.710
Great, thank you very much.

00:24:04.710 --> 00:24:05.317
&gt;&gt; No problem.

00:24:05.317 --> 00:24:09.845
&gt;&gt; Appreciate you coming in.

00:24:09.845 --> 00:24:11.503
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Now,

00:24:11.503 --> 00:24:14.661
we'll have Adam talk about metrics.

00:24:14.661 --> 00:24:16.880
&gt;&gt; Great, so thanks for having me.

00:24:16.880 --> 00:24:17.620
I'm happy to be here.

00:24:17.620 --> 00:24:20.640
I'm going to talk about metrics and
measurement and

00:24:20.640 --> 00:24:25.840
this whole group of issues that come
up as you're trying to start a company.

00:24:25.840 --> 00:24:31.100
And, one of the things, I think is really
interesting about this topic that is so

00:24:31.100 --> 00:24:36.240
much of what you have to do in
a startup is specific to the startup.

00:24:36.240 --> 00:24:37.542
You're going to have to get to
be an expert at some domain.

00:24:37.542 --> 00:24:40.840
You're going to have to get to be
better than anyone else at that domain.

00:24:40.840 --> 00:24:44.150
And a lot of what it takes to do that is,

00:24:44.150 --> 00:24:48.200
hard to teach in a class because it's
just different for every startup.

00:24:48.200 --> 00:24:52.480
But, metrics is this domain
that's kind of general,

00:24:52.480 --> 00:24:57.510
general enough to be useful
to almost all startups.

00:24:57.510 --> 00:25:00.570
So, I'm going to start
off with this example.

00:25:00.570 --> 00:25:06.028
So, this is the first version of
amazon.com from the early 90s.

00:25:06.028 --> 00:25:09.020
[COUGH] And,
I really like looking at these.

00:25:09.020 --> 00:25:10.979
You can look at the first versions
of lots of different products.

00:25:10.979 --> 00:25:15.335
It's a good reminder that it's much
easier to get these things started than

00:25:15.335 --> 00:25:16.230
you'd think.

00:25:16.230 --> 00:25:20.550
Even these companies that are massive
today, if you just go and

00:25:20.550 --> 00:25:25.220
look at how they started,
this is a good way

00:25:25.220 --> 00:25:29.670
to keep yourself in touch with
the reality of how companies get started.

00:25:29.670 --> 00:25:35.280
So, does anyone know why Amazon
decided to start with books?

00:25:38.552 --> 00:25:39.418
Yeah?

00:25:39.418 --> 00:25:41.660
&gt;&gt; Choice.

00:25:41.660 --> 00:25:46.480
&gt;&gt; Choice, yeah, yeah, yeah, so
that's actually, I think what you mean is

00:25:46.480 --> 00:25:49.890
that you have a lot of, you've got to
choose out from a lot of different books.

00:25:49.890 --> 00:25:52.088
Yeah, yeah, so
it wasn't just that they chose books,

00:25:52.088 --> 00:25:53.242
it was that-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:25:53.242 --> 00:25:55.291
&gt;&gt; It was books give you, so,

00:25:55.291 --> 00:26:00.016
Jeff Bezos wanted to a start a store and
one of the most important

00:26:00.016 --> 00:26:04.840
things when you're getting
started is differentiation.

00:26:04.840 --> 00:26:08.739
So, you don't have to make a product
that's going to appeal to everyone

00:26:08.739 --> 00:26:12.967
immediately, but, you need to make
a product that is going to appeal to some

00:26:12.967 --> 00:26:14.835
people more than anything else.

00:26:14.835 --> 00:26:19.114
So, you want to find some way
to differentiate, and usually,

00:26:19.114 --> 00:26:23.797
you want to do that by doing something
that's going to be easy for you but

00:26:23.797 --> 00:26:27.530
that's really hard for
all the existing companies.

00:26:27.530 --> 00:26:33.570
And, the Internet enabled this in
Amazon's case because brick and

00:26:33.570 --> 00:26:39.120
mortar stores have limited
space to hold their inventory.

00:26:39.120 --> 00:26:42.530
And so,
you realize that as on the Internet,

00:26:42.530 --> 00:26:47.660
you can have a big warehouse somewhere,
you can have items shipped from all over.

00:26:48.740 --> 00:26:51.379
This is something that enables you

00:26:51.379 --> 00:26:55.296
to offer much wider selection
than a normal store.

00:26:55.296 --> 00:26:59.300
So then, you go and
run through the thought experiment.

00:26:59.300 --> 00:27:05.540
You say, well, if what we can do is
provide better selection, then which

00:27:05.540 --> 00:27:11.930
category, what kind of store is going to
benefit the most from a greater selection?

00:27:11.930 --> 00:27:16.090
And it turns out that books is
a category where you have the most,

00:27:16.090 --> 00:27:19.040
there are more books than there
are other products, right?

00:27:19.040 --> 00:27:22.660
So if you look at like movies,
it's very expensive to make a movie.

00:27:22.660 --> 00:27:26.760
That's not a industry that
would benefit immediately from

00:27:26.760 --> 00:27:28.740
the Internet in the way that books would.

00:27:28.740 --> 00:27:33.445
So, I think there's a general
point here that measurement can

00:27:33.445 --> 00:27:36.034
turn a vague idea into a good idea.

00:27:36.034 --> 00:27:40.679
And, if you do a lot of these kind of
like back of the envelope calculations,

00:27:40.679 --> 00:27:44.817
it can really helps sharpen your ideas and
it can help you iterate and

00:27:44.817 --> 00:27:49.316
move through the space of ideas a lot
faster than if you have to go and like,

00:27:49.316 --> 00:27:50.717
build the prototype.

00:27:50.717 --> 00:27:54.065
You can measure what Amazon started and
they started to focus on movies and

00:27:54.065 --> 00:27:57.197
after a few years, they realized
that it wasn't working that well,

00:27:57.197 --> 00:28:01.120
then they're set back by a few years,
now then there's competition.

00:28:01.120 --> 00:28:02.620
These kind of metrics on,

00:28:02.620 --> 00:28:07.900
even in the realm of ideas can
really help you get started.

00:28:07.900 --> 00:28:12.980
And the one other thing I'd say about this
is that, I think using measurement here

00:28:12.980 --> 00:28:17.980
is very good for helping you figure out
what products are going to be possible or

00:28:17.980 --> 00:28:21.770
where you're going to be able to
provide a lot of value to a user?

00:28:21.770 --> 00:28:25.781
I actually don't think metrics works
that well for stuff like market size.

00:28:25.781 --> 00:28:31.256
So you just saw this example that Stewart,
they thought that the market size for

00:28:31.256 --> 00:28:34.210
chat was 100 million a year in revenue.

00:28:34.210 --> 00:28:35.920
It turns out to be 100 times bigger.

00:28:35.920 --> 00:28:37.800
It's just very hard to
figure those things out.

00:28:37.800 --> 00:28:38.485
I wouldn't spend a lot of time on that.

00:28:38.485 --> 00:28:41.257
I think you want to know
that whatever you're doing,

00:28:41.257 --> 00:28:45.340
someday there's some path for
it to eventually get really big.

00:28:45.340 --> 00:28:49.340
But, you want to really apply your metrics
to how can you differentiate your product,

00:28:49.340 --> 00:28:53.621
and what's going to be possible
that wasn't previously possible.

00:28:53.621 --> 00:28:59.540
So, okay so next, now you're building
your product and what should you measure?

00:28:59.540 --> 00:29:03.446
Just in any guesses, if you guys have
products, what are you measuring,

00:29:03.446 --> 00:29:05.281
what would you recommend, yeah?

00:29:05.281 --> 00:29:09.240
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; The amount of users, yeah, yeah, so

00:29:09.240 --> 00:29:11.556
what exactly would you
mean when you say that?

00:29:11.556 --> 00:29:19.525
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; Yeah,

00:29:19.525 --> 00:29:23.840
so how many people use a total and
how often they use it and go ahead.

00:29:23.840 --> 00:29:28.512
&gt;&gt; I think [INAUDIBLE] people share it but

00:29:28.512 --> 00:29:32.301
it's viral, [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; Like whether it's growing or

00:29:32.301 --> 00:29:33.570
whether they do something so
that it would grow, yeah?

00:29:33.570 --> 00:29:39.140
&gt;&gt; Total addressable market like
when you're getting started.

00:29:39.140 --> 00:29:40.306
&gt;&gt; Total addressable market, yep.

00:29:42.334 --> 00:29:43.447
Yeah?

00:29:43.447 --> 00:29:44.319
&gt;&gt; User attention.

00:29:44.319 --> 00:29:45.440
&gt;&gt; Yeah, user attention.

00:29:45.440 --> 00:29:46.768
So these are all great ideas.

00:29:46.768 --> 00:29:50.480
And, there's lots of things
that you can measure.

00:29:50.480 --> 00:29:52.230
As a startup, you really need to focus.

00:29:53.340 --> 00:30:00.790
So the core concept I would try to focus
on is, users that are getting value today.

00:30:00.790 --> 00:30:07.430
So, there's different indicators of this.

00:30:07.430 --> 00:30:10.300
You might say,
you want to focus on active users.

00:30:10.300 --> 00:30:13.850
People are going to be active,
if they're getting value.

00:30:13.850 --> 00:30:15.140
You might want to focus on revenue.

00:30:15.140 --> 00:30:17.100
That's a sign that people
are getting value.

00:30:17.100 --> 00:30:23.530
In the case of a marketplace, where you
have things like buyers and sellers,

00:30:23.530 --> 00:30:28.520
that's usually a case where it would
make sense to measure transactions.

00:30:28.520 --> 00:30:32.710
Any time you have these like
different groups, if you say, okay,

00:30:32.710 --> 00:30:35.810
we're going to measure buyers and
then we're going to measure sellers then,

00:30:35.810 --> 00:30:39.620
it's actually lot of stuff you can do that
will make things better for the buyers and

00:30:39.620 --> 00:30:42.490
worse for the sellers and
some of those things are good.

00:30:42.490 --> 00:30:46.836
And so measuring transactions
is a way to unify them and

00:30:46.836 --> 00:30:51.666
align your work on things that
are going to benefit both sides.

00:30:51.666 --> 00:30:55.148
So, if you have a two-sided market or
anything like that then,

00:30:55.148 --> 00:30:58.255
you want to measure transactions or
transaction values.

00:30:58.255 --> 00:30:59.740
So things like eBay,

00:30:59.740 --> 00:31:04.450
they'll measure total value of all
the goods that are sold on eBay.

00:31:04.450 --> 00:31:08.290
So to start,
this is kind of a quick thing.

00:31:08.290 --> 00:31:11.302
And you go a long way
just by measuring this.

00:31:11.302 --> 00:31:13.497
If you're not measuring anything,

00:31:13.497 --> 00:31:18.260
you're susceptible to all these cognitive
biases that Stewart talked about.

00:31:18.260 --> 00:31:19.470
There's this all these kinds of problems,

00:31:19.470 --> 00:31:20.920
you're just not going to
know what's going on.

00:31:22.407 --> 00:31:26.570
So next was most important thing
to think about is retention.

00:31:26.570 --> 00:31:30.460
So, first, I want to make sure
everyone understands what a cohort is.

00:31:30.460 --> 00:31:37.700
So, a cohort is all the users who first
use your product in a certain time window.

00:31:37.700 --> 00:31:40.330
So for a startup,
you might make the time window a week.

00:31:40.330 --> 00:31:44.550
So you could say, there's a cohort of
all the users who first use your product

00:31:44.550 --> 00:31:48.720
in the first week that it launched, and
you can track those users over time.

00:31:48.720 --> 00:31:52.240
There's a cohort of all the users that
first use the product a week later.

00:31:52.240 --> 00:31:55.746
And, you measure all these cohorts and
you can track them and

00:31:55.746 --> 00:31:58.856
this tells you what's
happening to users over time.

00:31:58.856 --> 00:32:02.082
And this is a really
important way to balance,

00:32:02.082 --> 00:32:07.590
you're just measuring the total value
that all the users are getting so

00:32:07.590 --> 00:32:13.460
This is a graph of total
of total cohort usage for

00:32:13.460 --> 00:32:16.990
a sort of relevantly old Internet product.

00:32:16.990 --> 00:32:22.528
So the blue line is users who
joined this product in 2004.

00:32:22.528 --> 00:32:25.301
And that's as you can see, and so

00:32:25.301 --> 00:32:30.530
this the y axis here is what
percent of them are still using it.

00:32:30.530 --> 00:32:31.720
And the x axis is time.

00:32:31.720 --> 00:32:33.882
So overtime you just follow the blue line.

00:32:33.882 --> 00:32:36.536
You can see usually over
month there's fewer and

00:32:36.536 --> 00:32:40.620
fewer people that joined the product
in 2004 that are still using it.

00:32:40.620 --> 00:32:45.330
And this is usually the same story for
all these different Cohort lines.

00:32:45.330 --> 00:32:48.910
So what do you think is going to
happen to this product in ten.

00:32:48.910 --> 00:32:53.530
This is about,
let's say this is about six years.

00:32:53.530 --> 00:32:54.630
The scale of this graph.

00:32:54.630 --> 00:32:56.792
What do you think is going
to happen six years later?

00:32:56.792 --> 00:33:01.153
Any guesses?

00:33:01.153 --> 00:33:02.405
&gt;&gt; So kind of the opposite.

00:33:02.405 --> 00:33:03.445
&gt;&gt; It's going to turn around.

00:33:03.445 --> 00:33:04.151
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:33:04.151 --> 00:33:06.180
&gt;&gt; Yeah, it could.

00:33:06.180 --> 00:33:07.900
I think something would need,

00:33:07.900 --> 00:33:11.525
something different would have
to happen to change that.

00:33:11.525 --> 00:33:12.800
&gt;&gt; No one using.

00:33:12.800 --> 00:33:13.670
&gt;&gt; No one will be using it.

00:33:13.670 --> 00:33:18.400
Yeah, so this is,
it's hard to know what would happen.

00:33:18.400 --> 00:33:21.942
I think probably everyone is
going to stop using their product.

00:33:21.942 --> 00:33:25.451
I think some companies will
manage to turn these around.

00:33:25.451 --> 00:33:29.465
But it's very hard too
because you get fewer and

00:33:29.465 --> 00:33:34.190
fewer, you get less and
less usage from your old users.

00:33:34.190 --> 00:33:37.354
It's also usually the old users
are what caused you to get new users.

00:33:37.354 --> 00:33:41.133
And so
if you have this problem it's pretty bad.

00:33:41.133 --> 00:33:43.180
This is a pretty scary place to be.

00:33:43.180 --> 00:33:47.060
Even if you can have a ton of users
you can have total usage growing.

00:33:47.060 --> 00:33:50.260
But if usage from every
cohort is declining

00:33:50.260 --> 00:33:53.019
then you're going to
run into big problems.

00:33:54.710 --> 00:33:57.950
So there's this idea called
the ring of fire which

00:33:57.950 --> 00:34:00.580
is a way to kind of visualize this.

00:34:00.580 --> 00:34:06.040
So unless there's some kind of lower bound
you're going to burn out all your users.

00:34:06.040 --> 00:34:11.100
And so here's the analogy,
imagine there's a big field of let's

00:34:11.100 --> 00:34:15.790
say of dead grass and
someone lights a match in the middle.

00:34:15.790 --> 00:34:19.440
You're going to get a fire and
the fire's going to grow and grow.

00:34:19.440 --> 00:34:23.649
But then in the middle of the fire you're
going to start to run out of fuel and

00:34:23.649 --> 00:34:24.963
it's going to burn out.

00:34:24.963 --> 00:34:26.440
And so you get this ring.

00:34:26.440 --> 00:34:28.610
And the ring is going to get bigger and
bigger.

00:34:28.610 --> 00:34:31.850
And in the center you're going to
burn out more and more of the fuel.

00:34:31.850 --> 00:34:34.874
And so the fire will get bigger and
bigger but

00:34:34.874 --> 00:34:40.360
eventually you're going to run out of the
whole field and the fires going to stop.

00:34:40.360 --> 00:34:41.770
You don't want this to happen.

00:34:43.340 --> 00:34:47.302
Here's an animation I made
to try to illustrate that.

00:34:47.302 --> 00:34:53.420
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So here's what you notice.

00:34:53.420 --> 00:34:56.520
So it looks great at first.

00:34:56.520 --> 00:35:00.650
There's more fire every frame
than the previous frame.

00:35:00.650 --> 00:35:03.350
But in the end there's nothing, right.

00:35:04.820 --> 00:35:08.210
So you don't want this to happen.

00:35:08.210 --> 00:35:10.952
And it's really important to
internalize this concept.

00:35:10.952 --> 00:35:14.988
Because if you don't you can fool yourself
and you can do a lot of things that

00:35:14.988 --> 00:35:18.014
are going to cause you to get more and
more usage over time.

00:35:18.014 --> 00:35:21.245
But at the expense of
the existing users and

00:35:21.245 --> 00:35:26.220
if you don't do this eventually
your company is going to die.

00:35:26.220 --> 00:35:32.320
I think some recent examples are I think
Groupon was a good example of this.

00:35:32.320 --> 00:35:34.885
They got a huge tons of usage,
all this merchants but

00:35:34.885 --> 00:35:38.048
it wasn't a good experience for
the merchants in the long term.

00:35:38.048 --> 00:35:40.600
And so they all stopped using it.

00:35:40.600 --> 00:35:44.680
And then I think more recently
Pokeman GO is another example.

00:35:44.680 --> 00:35:48.160
It got really big, lots of users but
it just kind of faded away.

00:35:48.160 --> 00:35:49.528
And so you don't want to build a fad.

00:35:49.528 --> 00:35:52.130
You want to build something
that's going to last.

00:35:52.130 --> 00:35:54.860
And so you need to make
sure that you're measuring

00:35:54.860 --> 00:35:56.770
the usage from the existing users.

00:35:56.770 --> 00:36:00.316
It's not just that you're
getting more users.

00:36:00.316 --> 00:36:05.240
So you need to make sure the existing
users keep using your product.

00:36:05.240 --> 00:36:10.160
Actually if this can go in reverse you're
in an extremely powerful position.

00:36:10.160 --> 00:36:13.290
There's a small number of products
where the cohort usage actually will

00:36:13.290 --> 00:36:14.970
increase over time.

00:36:14.970 --> 00:36:16.910
One example is Whatsapp.

00:36:16.910 --> 00:36:18.926
This is I think most messaging apps,

00:36:18.926 --> 00:36:23.357
as you get more of your friends on the app
there is more people to send messages to.

00:36:23.357 --> 00:36:28.490
And so they are able to get this
increase in cohorts usage overtime.

00:36:29.970 --> 00:36:32.020
Uber on the rider side.

00:36:32.020 --> 00:36:36.940
This is another good example as people
get used to Uber they use it more.

00:36:36.940 --> 00:36:39.530
As the pickup times come
down they use it more.

00:36:39.530 --> 00:36:41.280
As prices come down they use it more.

00:36:41.280 --> 00:36:45.530
And so
Uber's in a really strong position there.

00:36:45.530 --> 00:36:49.230
It's not true,
actually on the driver side.

00:36:49.230 --> 00:36:52.690
So Uber has problems on
driver side because they

00:36:52.690 --> 00:36:54.880
don't have this property there.

00:36:54.880 --> 00:36:58.460
And then Facebook you can see
the numbers they report publicly.

00:36:58.460 --> 00:37:03.100
They keep on their total usage is growing
faster than their number of users.

00:37:03.100 --> 00:37:06.182
So you know that they must have this
increase in usage per user overtime.

00:37:06.182 --> 00:37:08.681
So if you get to this,
this is very hard to get to this.

00:37:08.681 --> 00:37:13.014
But if you get to this point then
you're in a really strong position and

00:37:13.014 --> 00:37:15.960
one way to actually think
an idea is to whether.

00:37:17.740 --> 00:37:20.860
As the product gets bigger
as you get more users.

00:37:20.860 --> 00:37:25.452
As you develop the market is it
the kind of product where people

00:37:25.452 --> 00:37:27.584
would use it more over time.

00:37:27.584 --> 00:37:31.580
I think that's another lens you can
apply as you're considering ideas.

00:37:33.640 --> 00:37:38.540
Just one more data point, this venture
capitalist named Tom Tunguz did

00:37:38.540 --> 00:37:45.050
a study on what predicts valuations
that startups raise money at.

00:37:45.050 --> 00:37:47.043
And it's just a simple correlation study.

00:37:47.043 --> 00:37:50.003
And these are some different
factors in how much

00:37:50.003 --> 00:37:53.860
they correlate to the valuation
that a startup's going to get.

00:37:53.860 --> 00:37:59.646
First is growth of revenue,
and that's 0.18 correlation.

00:37:59.646 --> 00:38:05.450
Second is just how much
revenue they have in total.

00:38:05.450 --> 00:38:08.700
That actually Carl is a little
bit more strongly than growth.

00:38:08.700 --> 00:38:13.123
But by far the strongest correlation
is account expansion which means people

00:38:13.123 --> 00:38:14.628
spending more overtime.

00:38:14.628 --> 00:38:17.520
It's the same thing as
retention increasing overtime.

00:38:17.520 --> 00:38:20.980
So and
this correlation is extremely strong.

00:38:20.980 --> 00:38:27.970
So for a complex number like what
the valuation of a company is going to be.

00:38:27.970 --> 00:38:32.895
The fact that you can explain point
five four out of one of the variation

00:38:32.895 --> 00:38:35.662
in that number by the single variable.

00:38:35.662 --> 00:38:40.710
About whether the company is
increasing revenue per user over time.

00:38:40.710 --> 00:38:44.933
That's a sign, it's almost like
there's just two classes of companies.

00:38:44.933 --> 00:38:48.403
One class where there's
increasing revenue over time and

00:38:48.403 --> 00:38:53.520
another class that gets totally different
evaluations where this is not happening.

00:38:56.230 --> 00:38:59.340
So the main thing I just want you to
remember from today is you should measure

00:38:59.340 --> 00:39:00.020
your retention.

00:39:00.020 --> 00:39:04.770
That's going to lead you to do a lot
of things that will help you in all

00:39:04.770 --> 00:39:05.330
kinds of ways.

00:39:05.330 --> 00:39:07.040
It's going to help you,
it's going to help you grow.

00:39:07.040 --> 00:39:08.270
It's going to help you retain your users.

00:39:08.270 --> 00:39:10.510
It's going to help you build
a product that users really love.

00:39:13.390 --> 00:39:16.030
So next I just want to talk
about exponential growth.

00:39:16.030 --> 00:39:19.670
So retention is the most important
thing but it's not sufficient.

00:39:19.670 --> 00:39:22.537
You need to have a product that's
going to retain your users.

00:39:22.537 --> 00:39:27.163
But that's not necessarily enough to make
sure that you're going to be able to

00:39:27.163 --> 00:39:30.530
build a big product and reach everyone.

00:39:30.530 --> 00:39:34.400
You also need to make sure
that you are going to grow and

00:39:34.400 --> 00:39:39.302
by far the most powerful way to
grow is through exponential growth.

00:39:39.302 --> 00:39:43.548
This is just a few functions spotted
the red line is linear growth.

00:39:43.548 --> 00:39:46.628
And blue line is I think
is cubic growth and

00:39:46.628 --> 00:39:49.887
then the green line is exponential growth.

00:39:49.887 --> 00:39:54.809
And the linear growth path this
you could do these with few things

00:39:54.809 --> 00:39:57.490
like continually doing PR.

00:39:57.490 --> 00:39:59.230
You can get more and more users.

00:40:02.290 --> 00:40:05.547
Eventually, there's a limit to how
big you're going to be able to get.

00:40:05.547 --> 00:40:10.066
It gets expensive to run advertising,
it get's expensive to do these kinds

00:40:10.066 --> 00:40:13.590
of things that attract
a fixed number of users.

00:40:13.590 --> 00:40:19.450
But if you can do things that cause you
to get users in proportion to the number

00:40:19.450 --> 00:40:23.586
of users you already have, then that's
going to get you to exponential growth.

00:40:23.586 --> 00:40:28.440
So there are other places to learn
about what to do to grow faster.

00:40:28.440 --> 00:40:32.748
But as you're measuring your growth, you
want to make sure that the way you measure

00:40:32.748 --> 00:40:36.808
it is aligned with what you want, and
if what you want is exponential growth,

00:40:36.808 --> 00:40:39.510
then what you need to measure
is the rate of growth.

00:40:39.510 --> 00:40:43.710
So a good way to measure this is
just the percent increase per week

00:40:43.710 --> 00:40:47.020
in how much usage you have,
or how much revenue you have.

00:40:48.590 --> 00:40:52.416
This is a tweet from Paul Graham
that I especially agreed with.

00:40:52.416 --> 00:40:56.686
He said, a suggestion for really tough
founders is instead of graphing your

00:40:56.686 --> 00:40:59.370
revenue, graph the percent
growth per week.

00:40:59.370 --> 00:41:02.430
I don't actually know why he said that
this is for really tough founders.

00:41:02.430 --> 00:41:06.015
I actually think this is what's going to
make you really tough if you measure this.

00:41:06.015 --> 00:41:08.330
I think the causality
can go the other way.

00:41:08.330 --> 00:41:12.767
So this is a very
important thing to measure.

00:41:12.767 --> 00:41:15.388
The reason he says you
need to be tough is that,

00:41:15.388 --> 00:41:18.481
it's going to be hard to keep
up exponential growth, and

00:41:18.481 --> 00:41:22.333
it might not feel good to just see
this line that doesn't increase.

00:41:22.333 --> 00:41:25.593
But I mean you can also
measure your total revenue.

00:41:25.593 --> 00:41:27.239
There's no problem with doing that, but

00:41:27.239 --> 00:41:29.930
just make sure that you're measuring
the percent growth per week.

00:41:31.520 --> 00:41:32.664
Because a lot of the time,

00:41:32.664 --> 00:41:35.940
there's going to be things you can do
that don't cause exponential growth,

00:41:35.940 --> 00:41:38.489
that would actually help you
maybe more in the short term.

00:41:38.489 --> 00:41:42.279
And you want to focus on the stuff that's
going to cause the product to grow,

00:41:42.279 --> 00:41:44.390
in the short-term and in the long term.

00:41:44.390 --> 00:41:47.372
Which is basically things
like viral growth,

00:41:47.372 --> 00:41:52.652
getting users to invite more of their
friends, getting word of mouth spreading.

00:41:52.652 --> 00:41:56.007
Getting some kind of dynamic where
the bigger your product gets,

00:41:56.007 --> 00:41:58.396
the easier it is to attract
the next user to it.

00:42:01.242 --> 00:42:03.097
So next I want to talk about iteration.

00:42:03.097 --> 00:42:07.478
So one of the most important things, one
of the most important advantages you have

00:42:07.478 --> 00:42:11.050
as a start-up, is that you can
iterate faster than a big company.

00:42:12.130 --> 00:42:16.350
There's all kinds of things that cause
it to be hard for companies to change

00:42:16.350 --> 00:42:21.920
direction, and you just don't have these
forces when you're a small company.

00:42:21.920 --> 00:42:26.158
And so if you can iterate much
faster than other companies,

00:42:26.158 --> 00:42:28.882
then you can go through ideas faster.

00:42:28.882 --> 00:42:30.852
You can go through changes
to your product faster.

00:42:30.852 --> 00:42:33.286
You can go through changes
to improve retention faster.

00:42:33.286 --> 00:42:35.898
You can go through changes
to increase growth faster.

00:42:35.898 --> 00:42:40.090
It's going to add up to a really,
really strong position.

00:42:40.090 --> 00:42:43.706
But at the same time you have to be
iterating in the right direction.

00:42:43.706 --> 00:42:47.315
And so one of the reasons metrics, even
just simple metrics, are so important,

00:42:47.315 --> 00:42:50.518
is that they let you know whether
you're going in the right direction.

00:42:50.518 --> 00:42:55.335
They let you know whether what you're
doing is working, and they let you out

00:42:55.335 --> 00:42:59.710
of these different ways you can go
they kind of help you stay aligned.

00:43:00.820 --> 00:43:04.030
One other point I want to make,
I think this isn't mentioned too often,

00:43:04.030 --> 00:43:06.840
but you can measure how
fast you're iterating,

00:43:06.840 --> 00:43:09.620
and you can measure all these
components of iteration.

00:43:09.620 --> 00:43:14.010
So whether it's fully quantified or not,
you should have in your head, okay,

00:43:14.010 --> 00:43:18.852
how long did this cycle take us, and what
could we do to make the next cycle faster?

00:43:18.852 --> 00:43:24.170
At Quora, one of the metrics we track is,
how long does it take from an engineer

00:43:24.170 --> 00:43:29.086
hitting commits on their code,
to us being able to run the unit test, and

00:43:29.086 --> 00:43:32.412
then and
then get that code out into production.

00:43:32.412 --> 00:43:35.637
And we try to keep that in like
between 10 and 15 minutes,

00:43:35.637 --> 00:43:39.304
from a change in the code base to it
being totally out to all the users,

00:43:39.304 --> 00:43:42.670
because that allows people to
iterate just much much faster.

00:43:42.670 --> 00:43:44.519
And if we didn't track that metric,

00:43:44.519 --> 00:43:48.229
there's just a lot of forces that
naturally would cause it to get slower.

00:43:48.229 --> 00:43:51.423
I think a lot of companies end up,
they maybe start out like that,

00:43:51.423 --> 00:43:55.470
then they end up releasing daily, or
they go to weekly, or even monthly.

00:43:55.470 --> 00:44:00.387
I've heard of teams that are on like
every three months they do a release.

00:44:00.387 --> 00:44:05.228
And you just imagine,
how could you possibly iterate if

00:44:05.228 --> 00:44:10.080
your releases are only going monthly,
or even weekly?

00:44:10.080 --> 00:44:16.651
So next, there's a spectrum of
how seriously to take metrics,

00:44:16.651 --> 00:44:20.797
that I see a lot of companies stumble on.

00:44:20.797 --> 00:44:26.078
So on the one hand, there's not
taking your metrics seriously enough.

00:44:26.078 --> 00:44:29.761
And this might mean things like,
you don't have metrics.

00:44:29.761 --> 00:44:32.707
It might mean that you have metrics,
they're on some page, but

00:44:32.707 --> 00:44:33.916
no one ever looks at that.

00:44:33.916 --> 00:44:36.873
It might mean that you have metrics and
one person in the company looks at it.

00:44:36.873 --> 00:44:39.375
But no one else looks at it, and

00:44:39.375 --> 00:44:43.804
the issues that happen on
this end of the spectrum are,

00:44:43.804 --> 00:44:49.291
you kind of like, you can fool yourself,
you go around in circles,

00:44:49.291 --> 00:44:54.040
you just like lose sense of what
the right things to do are.

00:44:54.040 --> 00:44:56.407
And then there's another
end of the spectrum,

00:44:56.407 --> 00:44:59.022
where you can actually take
metrics too seriously.

00:44:59.022 --> 00:45:02.926
So you've gotta remember that
metrics are an abstraction.

00:45:02.926 --> 00:45:06.533
There is where you're actually
care about underneath is,

00:45:06.533 --> 00:45:11.379
let's say the value to the users, and
maybe the long-term value to the users.

00:45:11.379 --> 00:45:17.013
And so a good metric will be
strongly correlated with that,

00:45:17.013 --> 00:45:19.499
but it won't be perfect.

00:45:19.499 --> 00:45:23.789
And so you don't want to forget that
there's a reality under the metric that's

00:45:23.789 --> 00:45:25.570
not exactly the metric itself.

00:45:25.570 --> 00:45:27.444
And so
if you focus too much on the metrics,

00:45:27.444 --> 00:45:31.209
then you start to get into things like,
there's these ways to game the metrics.

00:45:31.209 --> 00:45:33.207
And we can make the metrics go up, but

00:45:33.207 --> 00:45:37.204
actually we're doing some things that
are going to hurt us long-term, and

00:45:37.204 --> 00:45:40.544
those things are going to
outweigh the short-term benefits.

00:45:40.544 --> 00:45:46.940
So I would really make sure
to be careful about that.

00:45:46.940 --> 00:45:52.008
I'd say, personally, I think the ideal
is to be somewhere in the middle,

00:45:52.008 --> 00:45:57.412
and I'd say you want to be very close to
taking the metrics extremely seriously.

00:45:57.412 --> 00:45:58.874
Not too seriously, but

00:45:58.874 --> 00:46:03.930
I think most companies bias toward not
being serious enough about their metrics.

00:46:03.930 --> 00:46:08.864
If you're in the right, if you're middle
in the right spot then you're learning,

00:46:08.864 --> 00:46:12.209
you're making good decisions,
and you're informed.

00:46:12.209 --> 00:46:15.923
But you're still aware that there
is a product underneath, and

00:46:15.923 --> 00:46:20.610
there is a strategy and there's more
to the world than just these metrics.

00:46:20.610 --> 00:46:23.406
I see a lot of times companies
will oscillate between these,

00:46:23.406 --> 00:46:26.902
like they'll say,
they'll get really focused on metrics and

00:46:26.902 --> 00:46:30.230
then that causes them to do some
things that they think are bad.

00:46:30.230 --> 00:46:32.254
And they're like these
metrics are terrible, and

00:46:32.254 --> 00:46:34.759
we're going to throw away our metrics,
not focus on them at all.

00:46:34.759 --> 00:46:37.406
And then you get into this world where you
don't actually know what you're doing, and

00:46:37.406 --> 00:46:38.930
there's no accountability.

00:46:38.930 --> 00:46:41.022
And so oscillation is not very productive,

00:46:41.022 --> 00:46:44.424
you want to just make sure you find
the right point and try to stick there.

00:46:46.639 --> 00:46:51.124
So finally, I want to just talk about some
of these psychological aspects that come

00:46:51.124 --> 00:46:52.540
with metrics.

00:46:52.540 --> 00:46:57.460
I think one of the big things that
come when you start measuring things,

00:46:57.460 --> 00:47:01.154
is you just get closer to
being in touch with reality.

00:47:01.154 --> 00:47:02.918
And that can be painful,

00:47:02.918 --> 00:47:08.560
because sometimes the metrics are going to
be worse than you thought they were.

00:47:08.560 --> 00:47:10.995
So sometimes they can be better than
you thought, and that's great, and

00:47:10.995 --> 00:47:11.792
everyone feels great.

00:47:11.792 --> 00:47:15.087
But a lot of times if you weren't
measuring something chances

00:47:15.087 --> 00:47:19.071
are you weren't even aware of it, it was
hard for you to make it good, and so

00:47:19.071 --> 00:47:21.000
it won't be good.

00:47:21.000 --> 00:47:24.760
And so
that means that as you set up metrics,

00:47:24.760 --> 00:47:29.610
there's going to be some pain,
and that can cause problems for

00:47:29.610 --> 00:47:34.100
companies, I even think at the leadership
level, this can get even worse.

00:47:34.100 --> 00:47:38.388
Actually, I think the majority of leaders
out there in the world rely on some

00:47:38.388 --> 00:47:41.947
amount of delusion they're
creating among their followers.

00:47:41.947 --> 00:47:46.609
To get them excited, to have them working
on this project in the first place, and

00:47:46.609 --> 00:47:48.610
metrics can get in the way of that.

00:47:49.670 --> 00:47:54.463
And so people get into this, then you get
into this question of like, okay, well,

00:47:54.463 --> 00:47:56.255
what should you do as a leader?

00:47:56.255 --> 00:48:00.570
And this is where I come out,
after thinking about this for a long time.

00:48:00.570 --> 00:48:05.144
I think you want to be learning from the
past, so you want everyone fully aware of

00:48:05.144 --> 00:48:08.623
what has happened, what has worked,
what has not worked.

00:48:08.623 --> 00:48:13.790
You want to be totally in touch with
the reality of where you are today.

00:48:13.790 --> 00:48:16.319
So if things are good,
you should know they're good.

00:48:16.319 --> 00:48:19.727
If things are not good, you should know
they're not good, you should know exactly

00:48:19.727 --> 00:48:23.060
where there are problems, and
where there are not problems.

00:48:23.060 --> 00:48:27.093
But then, those things are not in tension
with being optimistic about the future,

00:48:27.093 --> 00:48:29.234
you should be optimistic about the future.

00:48:29.234 --> 00:48:31.397
Because there's so much potential, and

00:48:31.397 --> 00:48:34.880
the reason you're doing a startup
is because of this potential.

00:48:34.880 --> 00:48:37.558
And if you don't feel
confident that the path

00:48:37.558 --> 00:48:41.714
you're on is able to get you to a place
that you feel optimistic about,

00:48:41.714 --> 00:48:45.394
then that's a good sign that you
might want to change the path.

00:48:45.394 --> 00:48:48.610
And you should do something about that,
because it's totally in your control.

00:48:48.610 --> 00:48:52.710
So there's not really a tension
between acknowledging that things

00:48:52.710 --> 00:48:57.940
are not in a great spot, and having people
not be optimistic about the future.

00:48:57.940 --> 00:49:01.400
So as long as you just make sure that
the reality is that, and I don't think you

00:49:01.400 --> 00:49:05.034
should make people optimistic about the
future in a way that you don't believe.

00:49:05.034 --> 00:49:08.670
I think you should do the things you need
to do, to get yourself to be confident,

00:49:08.670 --> 00:49:10.261
and optimistic about the future.

00:49:10.261 --> 00:49:13.891
And then it's easy to get
people to follow through that.

00:49:13.891 --> 00:49:17.195
So that's all I have, and
happy to take questions.

00:49:17.195 --> 00:49:19.250
